<description>
Render the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.

Input structure
A large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and
filled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one
object color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.

Rule
Ignore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the
smallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do
not count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all
square sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and
smaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background
separator column.

Key insight
The output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram
slot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "filled monochrome square objects s, grouped by side length after ignoring non-square noise and the lowest square when it is smallest",
  "dst": "left-to-right count-rank stack slots d",
  "match_cond": "a side group s contributes to slot d_k iff count(s) >= k",
  "transform": "draw each slot as nested upper-left square masks, larger sides first; append one bg separator column after every slot",
  "grid_shape": "height is largest side + 1; width is sum of slot widths plus one separator per slot"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 25-28 rows x 25-28 cols
  background: single dominant color (any of 0-9)
  squares:
    side_lengths: 3 consecutive sizes starting at 2 (i.e. 2,3,4) [observed]
    color_per_size: one unique non-bg color per side length; all 3 distinct
    count_per_size: 2-6 objects (after distractor removal, each size >= 1)
    placement: non-overlapping, min gap >= 1 cell between any two squares
  noise:
    color: one additional non-bg color distinct from all square colors
    pixels: 40-70 scattered, mostly isolated single pixels; some 4-connected clusters of 2-4
    density: ~7-10% of grid cells
    may be adjacent to squares
  distractor:
    condition: if the overall lowest-row square object has the smallest side length, it is excluded from counting
    count: 0 or 1 such distractor per grid
  output:
    height: largest_side + 1 (i.e. 5 when sides are 2,3,4)
    structure: left-to-right groups separated by 1-wide bg columns
    groups: max(counts) groups; group k includes sizes with count >= k
    rendering: each group draws squares upper-left aligned, largest first then smaller overlaid; group width = max side present in that group
    last column is a bg separator (trailing)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 3 consecutive side lengths starting at 2 (so 2,3,4). Assign a distinct non-bg color to each.
    2. Choose counts for each size (2-6 each). This defines the output.
    3. Decide distractor: with ~50% chance, add one extra smallest-side square that will be placed at the lowest row of the grid.
    4. Create a 25-28 x 25-28 grid filled with bg color.
    5. Place all square objects (including distractor) at random non-overlapping positions with gap >= 1. Ensure distractor's bottom row is the maximum bottom row among all placed squares.
    6. Scatter 40-70 noise pixels of a 4th distinct color randomly on bg cells (allowing occasional 4-adjacent clusters).
```
